WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.300
A practical buffer overflow prevention roadmap begins with design.

2
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:09.078
Preserve bounds and ownership information for as long as possible.

3
00:00:09.078 --> 00:00:17.679
Avoid pointer-only contracts when containers, views, strings, or buffer objects can express the real relationship more clearly.

4
00:00:17.679 --> 00:00:20.545
Prefer modern C++ abstractions by default.

5
00:00:20.545 --> 00:00:31.057
Use `std::array` for fixed-size storage, `std::vector` for dynamic contiguous storage, `std::string` for owned text, `std::span` for bounded

6
00:00:31.057 --> 00:00:36.313
non-owning views, and RAII to keep lifetime and cleanup visible.

7
00:00:36.313 --> 00:00:38.702
Validate sizes before memory operations.

8
00:00:38.702 --> 00:00:46.347
Check external length fields, maximum supported sizes, unit conversions, arithmetic, source availability, destination capacity, and output

9
00:00:46.347 --> 00:00:50.169
requirements before allocating, copying, parsing, or transforming data.

10
00:00:50.169 --> 00:00:52.080
Refactor legacy code incrementally.

11
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.903
Prioritize externally reachable parsers and buffer-heavy paths.

12
00:00:55.903 --> 00:01:01.159
Add wrappers, strengthen tests, replace ambiguous APIs, and document remaining risk.

13
00:01:01.159 --> 00:01:06.415
Prevention improves when every change makes the contract easier to review.

14
00:01:06.415 --> 00:01:11.193
Finally, test, harden, verify, and revisit the baseline over time.

15
00:01:11.193 --> 00:01:18.360
Use boundary tests, sanitizers, defensive fuzzing, compiler diagnostics, hardened release profiles, artifact verification, and governance.

16
00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:26.960
Buffer overflow prevention is not one technique; it is disciplined engineering across design, implementation, testing, build, and release.
